  "status": "exported",
  "rationale": "This Burundi is a dense, high-grown washed Bourbon, close kin to the Rwanda Rusizi Gaseke: same process, same variety, same bean density, and the same charge and warm-up. So version 1 keeps the Rwanda schedule exactly: the same fan steps and the same four heater cuts at the same bean temperatures. On the Rwanda, development of about 2:15 to 2:30 after first crack tasted balanced and best; under about 1:40 it was sour, and past about 3:10 roasty. This plan's 2:32 sits in that window and suits a City+ roast that keeps the tea-like brightness and honey sweetness. First crack can land up to a minute away from the prediction on a bean roasted here for the first time, so nothing else changes yet; the three batches test development instead, and this first session finds the Burundi's own best development time.",
  "roast_level_target": "City+",
  "contrast_lever_designed": {
    "key": "dev_seconds",
    "values": [
      132,
      152,
      172
    ],
    "why": "Development is not yet settled for THIS bean (envelope and verdicts are borrowed from Rwanda Rusizi Gaseke). On that prior, ~67-70s was very sour/grassy, ~134-152s was balanced and preferred, and ~190s turned roasty/astringent. Bracketing dev around the proven 152s finds this Burundi's own sweet spot in one session."
  },
